examples.solution_text:
(a) The gradient depends only on $x$. Changing $y$ within a column leaves the gradient unchanged.
(b) Horizontal elements have gradient $0$.
$\begin{aligned}1-\frac{x}{2}&=0\\x&=2\end{aligned}$
The zero-gradient isocline is $x=2$.
(c) Start at $(0,1)$ and follow the elements in both directions. The curve rises for $x<2$ and falls for $x>2$, giving a maximum. Integration confirms its position.
$\begin{aligned}y&=\int\left(1-\frac{x}{2}\right)\,dx\\y&=x-\frac{x^2}{4}+C\end{aligned}$
Using $(0,1)$ gives $C=1$. At $x=2$, $y=2$, so the maximum is $(2,2)$.
