examples.solution_text:
(a) Plot $(0,3)$ and follow the elements in both directions. The tangent is horizontal there. On the positive branch, gradients are negative to the left and positive to the right, so the curve has a minimum at $(0,3)$.
(b) Differentiate the proposed function.
$\begin{aligned}\dfrac{dy}{dx}&=\frac12(x^2+9)^{-1/2}(2x)\\&=\frac{x}{\sqrt{x^2+9}}\\&=\frac{x}{y}\end{aligned}$
Also, $y(0)=\sqrt{9}=3$. The function satisfies the differential equation and the initial condition.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=8mm,y=8mm,every node/.style={text=black,font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\definecolor{solutioncurve}{HTML}{268CFF}
\draw[color=black,->] (-3.6,0)--(3.6,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[color=black,->] (0,-1.6)--(0,5) node[above] {$y$};
\foreach \t in {-3,-2,-1,1,2,3} {\path (\t,-0.35) node[fill=white,inner sep=1pt,font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont] {$\t$};}
\foreach \t in {-1,1,2,3,4} {\path (-0.35,\t) node[fill=white,inner sep=1pt,font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont] {$\t$};}
\foreach \a in {-3,-2,-1,0,1,2,3} {\foreach \b in {-1,1,2,3,4} {\pgfmathsetmacro{\m}{\a/\b}\pgfmathsetmacro{\h}{0.22/sqrt(1+\m*\m)}\draw[color=black] ({\a-\h},{\b-\m*\h})--({\a+\h},{\b+\m*\h});}}
\foreach \a in {-3,-2,-1,1,2,3} {\draw[color=black] (\a,-0.22)--(\a,0.22);}
\draw[color=solutioncurve,thick,<->,domain=-3.3:3.3,samples=80,variable=\x] plot ({\x},{sqrt(\x*\x+9)});
\fill[color=black] (0,3) circle (1.5pt);
\path (0,3) node[above right=4pt,fill=white,inner sep=1pt] {$(0,3)$};
\path (0,6.5) node[text=solutioncurve] {$y=\sqrt{x^2+9}$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

(a) Substitute the line into the right-hand side.
$\begin{aligned}-2x-y&=-2x-(2-2x)\\&=-2\end{aligned}$
The field gradient is constant along the line, so it is an isocline. The line's own derivative is also $-2$, so it is a solution curve.
(b) At $(0,3)$ the gradient is $-2(0)-3=-3$, so the curve initially falls.
Its vertical separation from the straight solution is
$\begin{aligned}y-(2-2x)&=e^{-x}\\&>0.\end{aligned}$
It stays above the line and never meets it. As $x$ increases, $e^{-x}$ approaches $0$, so $y=2-2x$ is an oblique asymptote.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=8mm,y=8mm,every node/.style={text=black,font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\definecolor{solutioncurve}{HTML}{268CFF}
\definecolor{straightsolution}{HTML}{EF6068}
\draw[color=black,->] (-0.5,0)--(3.6,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[color=black,->] (0,-4.8)--(0,3.8) node[above] {$y$};
\foreach \t in {1,2,3} {\path (\t,-0.35) node[fill=white,inner sep=1pt,font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont] {$\t$};}
\foreach \t in {-4,-2,2,3} {\path (-0.3,\t) node[fill=white,inner sep=1pt,font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont] {$\t$};}
\foreach \a in {0,1,2,3} {\foreach \b in {-4,-3,-2,-1,0,1,2,3} {\pgfmathsetmacro{\m}{-2*\a-\b}\pgfmathsetmacro{\h}{0.18/sqrt(1+\m*\m)}\draw[color=black] ({\a-\h},{\b-\m*\h})--({\a+\h},{\b+\m*\h});}}
\draw[color=straightsolution,dashed,domain=0:3.2,variable=\x] plot ({\x},{2-2*\x});
\draw[color=solutioncurve,thick,->,domain=0:3.2,samples=80,variable=\x] plot ({\x},{2-2*\x+exp(-\x)});
\fill[color=black] (0,3) circle (1.5pt);
\path (0,3) node[right=4pt,fill=white,inner sep=1pt] {$(0,3)$};
\path (4,-2.5) node[right,text=straightsolution] {$y=2-2x$};
\draw[color=straightsolution] (3,-4)--(4,-2.8);
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

examples.question_text:
The slope field represents $\dfrac{dy}{dx}=(y-1)(y+2)$.
(a) Explain why its horizontal rows contain identical gradients.
(b) Find the constant solutions.
(c) Describe the solution through $(0,0)$ as $x$ increases.
(d) Describe the direction and changing steepness of the solution through $(0,2)$.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=9mm,y=9mm,every node/.style={text=black,font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\draw[color=black,->] (-1.5,0)--(3.6,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[color=black,->] (0,-3.6)--(0,3.6) node[above] {$y$};
\foreach \t in {-1,1,2,3} {\path (\t,-0.35) node[fill=white,inner sep=1pt,font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont] {$\t$};}
\foreach \t in {-3,-2,-1,1,2,3} {\path (-0.35,\t) node[fill=white,inner sep=1pt,font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont] {$\t$};}
\foreach \a in {-1,0,1,2,3} {\foreach \b in {-3,-2,-1,0,1,2,3} {\pgfmathsetmacro{\m}{(\b-1)*(\b+2)}\pgfmathsetmacro{\h}{0.22/sqrt(1+\m*\m)}\draw[color=black] ({\a-\h},{\b-\m*\h})--({\a+\h},{\b+\m*\h});}}
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

examples.solution_text:
(a) The gradient depends only on $y$. Changing $x$ along a row leaves the gradient unchanged.
(b) A constant solution has derivative $0$.
$\begin{aligned}(y-1)(y+2)&=0\end{aligned}$
The constant solutions are $y=1$ and $y=-2$. Each horizontal line has the same gradient as its field elements.
(c) For $-2<y<1$, the first factor is negative and the second positive, so the curve falls. This equation has unique solutions through points, so the curve through $(0,0)$ cannot cross either constant solution.
It decreases towards $-2$. A limiting value strictly between $-2$ and $1$ would leave a nonzero negative gradient, so the curve could not settle there.
(d) For $y>1$, both factors are positive and their product increases as $y$ rises. The curve through $(0,2)$ rises and becomes steeper. It increases without bound at a finite positive value of $x$.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=9mm,y=9mm,every node/.style={text=black,font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\definecolor{solutioncurve}{HTML}{268CFF}
\definecolor{equilibrium}{HTML}{EF6068}
\draw[color=black,->] (-1.5,0)--(3.6,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[color=black,->] (0,-3.6)--(0,3.6) node[above] {$y$};
\foreach \t in {-1,1,2,3} {\path (\t,-0.35) node[fill=white,inner sep=1pt,font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont] {$\t$};}
\foreach \t in {-3,-2,-1,1,2,3} {\path (-0.35,\t) node[fill=white,inner sep=1pt,font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont] {$\t$};}
\foreach \a in {-1,0,1,2,3} {\foreach \b in {-3,-2,-1,0,1,2,3} {\pgfmathsetmacro{\m}{(\b-1)*(\b+2)}\pgfmathsetmacro{\h}{0.22/sqrt(1+\m*\m)}\draw[color=black] ({\a-\h},{\b-\m*\h})--({\a+\h},{\b+\m*\h});}}
\draw[color=equilibrium,dashed] (-1.2,1)--(3.2,1);
\draw[color=equilibrium,dashed] (-1.2,-2)--(3.2,-2);
\draw[color=solutioncurve,thick,->,domain=0:3.2,samples=80,variable=\x] plot ({\x},{(1-exp(3*\x))/(1+exp(3*\x)/2)});
\draw[color=solutioncurve,thick,->,domain=0:0.19,samples=50,variable=\x] plot ({\x},{(1+exp(3*\x)/2)/(1-exp(3*\x)/4)});
\fill[color=black] (0,0) circle (1.5pt);
\fill[color=black] (0,2) circle (1.5pt);
\path (0,0) node[above right=4pt,fill=white,inner sep=1pt] {$(0,0)$};
\path (0,2) node[right=5pt,fill=white,inner sep=1pt] {$(0,2)$};
\path (3.25,1) node[right,text=equilibrium] {$y=1$};
\path (3.25,-2) node[right,text=equilibrium] {$y=-2$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
